h1. 4. Solution Architecture

{code:none}
DevSecOpsJenkinsLibrary/           <- library repository root
├── documentation.confluence       <- this document, Confluence wiki markup, the single source
├── documentation.html             <- the same documentation as a single HTML page, generated from it
├── vars/                          <- Jenkins global variables: the entry points and the composition root
│   ├── devSecOpsPipeline.groovy               <- entry point: all thirteen stages
│   ├── devSecOpsSecurityPipeline.groovy       <- entry point: static analysis part
│   ├── devSecOpsExtendedPipeline.groovy       <- entry point: deployment and test part
│   ├── devSecOpsSASTScanningPipeline.groovy   <- entry point: SAST only
│   ├── devSecOpsSteps.groovy      <- one method per stage, included by every entry point
│   └── devSecOpsApi.groovy        <- composition root: builds every service and exposes the helper methods
├── src/com/bbh/
│   ├── core/                               <- the domain core, no Jenkins plugin knowledge
│   │   ├── PipelineState.groovy            <- everything the stages record: results, counts, coverage, links
│   │   ├── PolicyEngine.groovy             <- library policy, orange stages and the stage result log
│   │   ├── ReleaseGate.groovy              <- blocks the Nexus release and the QC deployment when a stage is not green
│   │   └── OsHelper.groovy
│   ├── config/ConfigLoader.groovy          <- portal documents -> PipelineState and the run-state file pipeline-config.yaml
│   ├── config/PortalConfigReader.groovy    <- reads the configuration of the pipeline keys from the portal over HTTPS
│   ├── build/
│   │   ├── BuildService.groovy             <- build, unit tests, coverage, remote test jobs
│   │   ├── BuildRunner.groovy              <- one runner for the Gradle and Maven commands
│   │   └── ManifestBuildVerifier.groovy    <- adapter: the GoldenFix pre-check build, one check per changed manifest
│   ├── scanner/                            <- adapters for the scanners
│   │   ├── AppScanService.groovy           <- HCL AppScan SAST and DAST
│   │   ├── NexusIqService.groovy           <- Nexus IQ scan, calls the remediation port on a violation
│   │   ├── NexusIqGoldenFixSource.groovy   <- adapter: Nexus IQ report + Component Remediation REST API
│   │   └── SonarService.groovy
│   ├── remediation/                        <- GoldenFix, ports and adapters
│   │   ├── GoldenFixService.groovy         <- application service: fetch -> patch manifests -> commit/push -> pull request
│   │   ├── model/GoldenFix.groovy          <- the remediation domain: the fix record, version semantics and the version choice
│   │   ├── port/DependencyRemediation.groovy  <- inbound port used by NexusIqService
│   │   ├── port/GoldenFixSource.groovy        <- outbound port: where the proposed versions come from
│   │   ├── port/SourceRepository.groovy       <- outbound port: source tree, commit, push
│   │   ├── port/PullRequestPublisher.groovy   <- outbound port: pull request
│   │   ├── port/ManifestUpdater.groovy        <- outbound port: one build manifest format
│   │   ├── port/BuildVerifier.groovy          <- outbound port: pre-check build of the proposed versions
│   │   └── updater/                        <- MavenPomUpdater, GradleUpdater, NpmPackageJsonUpdater, PipUpdater, PubUpdater
│   ├── scm/                                <- adapters: GitSourceRepository (git worktree), Bitbucket pull requests
│   ├── deploy/                             <- adapters: VmDeployService (SSH, UrbanCode Deploy), OpenshiftService
│   ├── metrics/
│   │   ├── InfluxDbService.groovy          <- adapter: writes the batch to InfluxDB
│   │   ├── PipelineMetrics.groovy          <- builds every measurement from the pipeline state
│   │   └── MetricLine.groovy               <- line protocol formatting and escaping
│   ├── report/HtmlReportService.groovy     <- one report template, the variant only selects the stage layout
│   └── utils/                              <- RestClient (curl JSON client), AppScanReportParser, FlutterSonarReports, BuildUtils, FlutterUtils
├── tools/build-documentation-html.py       <- renders documentation.confluence into documentation.html
├── test/                          <- the library test suite, see section 17
│   ├── run-all.sh                 <- one command: static checks, compilation, sandbox scenarios, demo reports
│   ├── checks/                    <- static checks of the sources
│   └── sandbox/                   <- the library running under the real Jenkins script sandbox
└── examples/
    ├── README.md                  <- how to split the pipeline into a static and an extended job
    ├── CertScanner/               <- a complete two project example: the Jenkinsfile with two pipeline keys
    └── reports/                   <- generated demo reports, one HTML page per pipeline and outcome
{code}

*How the parts fit together (hexagonal):* {{core}} holds the state, the policy and the release gate and knows nothing about Jenkins plugins. The GoldenFix feature is a full hexagon: the application service in {{remediation}} talks only to the ports in {{remediation/port}}, the domain rules (which version to take, how versions compare) live in {{remediation/model}}, and the adapters sit in {{scanner}} (Nexus IQ), {{scm}} (git, Bitbucket) and {{remediation/updater}} (one per manifest format). {{vars/devSecOpsApi.groovy}} is the single composition root that assembles every service; the services in {{scanner}}, {{build}}, {{deploy}}, {{metrics}} and {{report}} are adapters around the core, each receiving the pipeline script object and translating between the tools and the state. A check in the test suite enforces these dependency directions.

*How the parts fit together (hexagonal):* {{core}} holds the state, the policy and the release gate and knows nothing about Jenkins plugins. The GoldenFix feature is a full hexagon: the application service in {{remediation}} talks only to the ports in {{remediation/port}}, and the adapters live in {{scanner}} (Nexus IQ), {{scm}} (git, Bitbucket) and {{remediation/updater}} (manifest formats). {{vars/devSecOpsApi.groovy}} and {{wiring/GoldenFixFactory.groovy}} are the composition roots that assemble the services; the remaining services in {{scanner}}, {{build}}, {{deploy}}, {{metrics}} and {{report}} are adapters around the core, each of them receiving the pipeline script object and translating between the tools and the state.

*Config split:*

||Source||Who controls it||Contains||
|Global Settings of the DevSecOps portal|DevSecOps team|Security thresholds, required coverage, timeouts, tool defaults ({{defaults}}) and the platform values: AppScan, proxy and Nexus addresses, the iOS build agent ({{platform}})|
|The service in the DevSecOps portal|Project team, in the portal|Service settings (appId, servers, test jobs, deploy targets), the pipeline type, the agent labels and the linked security or extended job|
|One Jenkins global environment variable|Jenkins administrators|The address of the portal ({{DSO_PORTAL_URL}})|

Projects *cannot* override the {{defaults}}. Thresholds for SAST, SCA, Nexus IQ, DAST and the required line coverage exist in one place only, the Global Settings of the portal, and the next build of every pipeline uses a changed value.

h3. How a pipeline reads its configuration

# The entry point calls {{devSecOpsApi.configure('<variant>', [pipelineKey: '<key>'])}} before {{pipeline {}}}. The keys are checked before anything runs; the console and every message show only a hint of a key, its first eight and last four characters.
# Outside a node the read runs on an agent of the label {{DSO_PORTAL_AGENT}} (default {{linux-agent}}) and must finish within five minutes. A custom Jenkinsfile that calls {{configure()}} inside a node of a Linux or macOS agent reads on that node.
# A short {{sh}} script sends one {{GET <DSO_PORTAL_URL>/api/dso/config/<key>?format=json}} per key with {{curl}}, asking again twice, five seconds apart, when the portal cannot be reached or answers 408, 429 or 5xx. The keys reach the script only through its environment, and the answers go to a new directory below the workspace's {{@tmp}} folder, deleted afterwards. The portal renders the configuration at that moment from its database and records the key's last use; the library holds no account and changes nothing.
# The library checks every answer: the portal issued the key, the key is active, the answer is a configuration, its pipeline type is the one of the entry point, all keys belong to one product and name each service once. The first key is the primary project, as the first name of {{projectNames}} was.
# In the Monitor stage {{ConfigLoader.initialize()}} merges every service with the global defaults exactly as {{defaults.yaml}} was merged under {{config.yaml}}, and writes the services to {{pipeline-config.yaml}}, the run-state file in which the snapshot stages record their build tags.
# An extended pipeline whose key names a security job copies {{pipeline-config.yaml}} and {{release-gate.json}} from the last successful build of that job and takes from the copy only the run-time tags: {{deploy.openshift.rd.buildTag}}, {{deploy.openshift.rd.internalDockerUrl}}, the VM delivery version in {{delivery}}, {{delivery.buildTagAndroid}} and {{delivery.buildTagIOS}}. Each must be a plain tag of letters, digits and {{. _ : / @ + -}}.

h3. Differences from config.yaml

The portal integration keeps every stage, scanner, deployment, report, metric, release gate and GoldenFix pull request as it was with {{config.yaml}} and {{defaults.yaml}}, except for these differences, each forced by having no {{config.yaml}} or by reading the portal only:

# The configuration lives in the portal, not in the repository: there is no configuration per branch, pull request or commit, a replayed build uses today's portal values, and the release and develop jobs of one service share one pipeline per type. The console, the report header and {{build_evidence}} record the key hint, the time the portal rendered the configuration (the time of the read) and the sha256 hint of the answer.
# A failure before {{pipeline {}}} (no key, a bad, unknown or invalidated key, a type or product mismatch, an unreachable portal) ends the build without a report, {{release-gate.json}} or InfluxDB point. {{build_duration}} and the DORA duration include the read.
# The archived run-state file is {{pipeline-config.yaml}}, not {{config.yaml}}; the first extended build after the cutover needs one security build made by the new library.
# The extended pipeline uses the services of its own key plus the run-time tags of the security build, not the whole {{config.yaml}} the security build archived.
# Keys the portal does not model are gone from {{getCFG()}}, such as custom keys a team added to {{config.yaml}}. {{tests.<suite>.defaults}}, test jobs given as a bare URL and {{urls}} lists are expanded into jobs when a service is migrated. {{jobs[].auth}} maps other than credential IDs, OpenShift {{appName}}, {{imageNamespace}}, {{cluster}} and {{credentialsId}} (read only by the unused {{nexusDelivery}} method) and the keys no code reads ({{asoc.keySecret}}, {{sast.scanName}}, {{sca.scanName}}, {{dast.scanName}}, {{build.maven.javaPath}}) are not modelled. {{jenkins.pipeline.extendedPipeline}} is in the documents of security pipelines only, so a custom Jenkinsfile that calls {{devSecOpsApi.runExtendedPipeline()}} configures itself with the key of the security pipeline.
# Selecting projects through {{PROJECT_NAMES}} or {{PROJECT_NAME}} without keys is gone; the keys decide.
# Messages that named {{config.yaml}} or {{defaults.yaml}} now name the portal, also where they reach the report, {{release-gate.json}} and InfluxDB. A {{securityPipeline}} in the Jenkinsfile of a full, security or SAST pipeline is ignored, which shows the Nexus IQ and SonarQube summary rows it used to hide.
# Nexus IQ report links appear for every service, because the Nexus IQ server address is a global setting; GoldenFix stays governed by its own switch per service.
# Every build depends on the portal at its start: the agents of the {{DSO_PORTAL_AGENT}} label need HTTPS access to it.

